      "question": {
        ":question/id": "975aa8ad-f12d-5f97-b8cb-953306ec2e7a",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{3 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =3 x^{2} + 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6 x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =6 x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3 x^{2} + 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(3 x^{2} + 1)}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\ln(u)|_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\ln(4)-\\ln(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\log{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
